# The product guide

`docs/guide/` is the operator's manual: one page per operation, every control
explained, illustrated from the running app. A change to the interface that
leaves it behind is an unfinished change.

It is written for the agent running their book, not for a developer. Anything
about architecture, commands or the schema belongs in `docs/technical/` instead.

| Page | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `README.md` | Contents. Every other page is linked from here |
| `install-and-first-run.md` | Installing, creating the book, unlocking, locking, quitting |
| `getting-around.md` | The sidebar, routes, search, badges, the dashboard |
| `import-your-book.md` | The import screen end to end, including the recognised-column tables |
| `clients.md` | The clients list and the client form |
| `insured-members.md` | Members on a client and on a policy |
| `policies.md` | The policies list, the policy form, statuses |
| `renewals.md` | The renewals desk, renewing, policy history |
| `insurers-and-plans.md` | The insurers screen |
| `settings.md` | The settings screen except backups |
| `backups-and-data.md` | Backups, restoring, moving machines, the data folder |
| `reference.md` | Lookup tables, glossary, questions |

Write it under `documentation-style`: present tense, assertive, no changelog
framing. Replace the paragraph that described the old behaviour instead of
adding a note beside it.

## When the interface changes

1. Edit the page above that owns the screen, and only that page. A new
   operation gets its own page, linked from `README.md`.
2. Re-photograph the app:

   ```bash
   npm run docs:screenshots
   ```

3. Prove the guide still covers everything:

   ```bash
   npm run docs:check
   ```

Both commands are also run by `.github/workflows/docs.yml`, so skipping them
fails the build rather than shipping a stale guide.

## How the screenshots work

`scripts/docs/capture.mjs` serves the real app with Vite, answers the Rust side
from the demo book in `scripts/docs/fixtures.mjs`, freezes the clock at
`TODAY`, walks each route, opens each dialog, and sizes the window to the
content. Every picture in the guide comes from that run — none is taken by hand.

- **A new screen or dialog**: add a shot to the `shots` array in
  `capture.mjs` with its caption, and reference `screenshots/<name>.png` from
  the page that owns it. The check fails on a shot that no one shows and on an
  image the capture script does not produce.
- **A new command**: answer it in `scripts/docs/mock-tauri.mjs`. An unanswered
  command throws, and the shot lands on an error state.
- **New data on screen**: extend `fixtures.mjs` rather than special-casing the
  page. Dashboard totals, tab counts and list contents are all derived from
  that one dataset, so they agree with each other.
- Keep the demo book fictional, and keep it shaped like real output — client
  codes in the app's `CL-00001` form, plausible Indian insurers and cities. No
  real client names, numbers or policy numbers ever go into a screenshot.

## What `docs:check` enforces

- Every page in `docs/guide/` is linked from `docs/guide/README.md`.
- Every route in `src/App.tsx` is named somewhere in the guide.
- Every sidebar item in `src/components/AppShell.tsx` is named in the guide.
- Every `<Button>` label in `src/pages/**` is explained, except the generic ones
  listed in `scripts/docs/check-guide.mjs`.
- The screenshots on disk, the screenshots in the guide, and the shots the
  capture script takes are the same set.

Add a label to the generic list only when it genuinely carries no meaning of its
own. Documenting the operation is the cheaper fix.
